  flushes the deque and lets a full [38;5;153mlimit[39m-sized burst through at once. On a gateway process that runs for weeks, those
  aren't edge cases.

  The reviewer's actual need — a limiter decision you can line up against a log line — doesn't require the window clock
  to change. Whatever logs the decision should stamp it with [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at the call site. Nothing in this repo calls
  [38;5;153mallow()[39m yet outside tests, so there's no code to change for that today; it's a constraint on the caller, not on the
  limiter. I'm not storing a [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pair either — nothing reads the stored timestamps back, so that's memory
  and complexity for a diagnostic nobody has asked for yet.

  [1mItem 3 — no backend interface.[0m The trigger for extracting an interface is the second implementation, not the
  anticipation of one. Right now there's one implementation, no Redis dependency, and no caller. Designing against the
  deque would also produce the wrong shape: a correct Redis sliding window needs atomicity ([38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m/[38;5;153mZADD[39m
  in one Lua script), key TTLs, and a fail-open-or-closed policy when Redis is unreachable. An interface derived from a
  three-line deque expresses none of that, so the Redis work would rewrite it anyway — we'd pay for the abstraction
  twice and carry the indirection in between. The deque touches three lines; extracting it later is mechanical.

  The thing that would change my mind on item 3 is a committed Redis migration, because then I'd design the interface
  against Redis's semantics and adapt the deque to fit, which is the opposite of what the review asked for. If that
  lands, reopen it.

